this month Congress will decide the fate  of the export-import bank XM is a  federal agency that subsidizes US  exports by extending taxpayer backed  financing to overseas buyers including  foreign governments and companies Exim  charter expires September 30th but  Congress has taken up a bill to renew  the agency for ten more years without  Exxon's loans and loan guarantees the  sponsors argue US exporters would suffer  from a lack of financing but that's not  what recent history has shown us in 2015  conservative opposition caused the  bank's charter to lapse and its board to  lose a quorum so for nearly four years  Exim couldn't finance large deals what  happened to financing for aircraft which  is XM's main line of business it  blossomed like never before  you see Boeing assembled a consortium of  lenders and insurers who found all sorts  of innovative ways to finance planes  that's not surprising when government  steps out of the way the market steps in  Exum's clients do like government  financing but that doesn't mean they  need it what do you think should  Congress reauthorize export-import bank  or kill it let us know in our poll also  let us know what other topics you'd like  scholars to cover in 60 seconds and be  sure to LIKE and subscribe for more  research and videos from AEI
 

